(project tuna-routes
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: routes as paths - endpoints become data in the path index. A route is a tree_paths entry under the reserved route/ namespace whose value is a JSON record naming a program and/or a byte-value template. Research question: for a substrate whose path index + prefix grants already are an ACL'd routing table, does data-first routing beat handler-code routing?")
 (status planned)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section routes
  (doc "Publishing an endpoint is a store write - journaled, versioned (CAS), grant-gated, rewritable - with no rebuild and no redeploy. New OCaml only when the prim surface itself grows.")
  (status planned)
  (subsection laws
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      1. ROUTES ARE DATA. route/<site-path> maps site path /<site-path>
         to a record byte value: {"method": GET or POST or any,
         "template":hash or null,"program":hash or null,"content_type":str,
         "grant_prefix":str or null}. Writes go through the normal path
         put/cas/delete ops; the ops chain audits every change.
      2. STATIC WINS. Reserved surfaces (Pages, /api/*, /health,
         /login, /logout) match first, always. Publishing a route whose
         site-path collides with a reserved path is a journaled denial.
         Reserved list is pinned here, not in code, and may only grow
         by book change: api, health, login, logout, frag, grants,
         programs, runs, repl, value, route, static.
      3. THE ROUTE RECORD IS THE CAPABILITY. Template routes serve
         bytes to any caller, anonymous included - a public site needs
         it. Program routes execute the stored program as a
         daemon-attributed run whose only capability is the record's
         own grant_prefix; the visitor carries nothing. Authenticated
         invokers get their identity on the run row.
      4. RESULT CONTRACT. A program route's run must end in a value
         hash (+ the record's content_type); anything else answers 500
         with the run id in the body. Debuggability beats silence.
      5. DISPATCH. Match record method against request method; no
         match -> next candidate; nothing matches -> plain 404 (routes
         serve anonymous by design; no login redirect). |))))
  (subsection surface
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      POST /api/route/put    {path, record, expected_version?} -> {ok, version, value_hash}
      POST /api/route/delete {path, expected_version}
      GET  /api/route/get?path=...      GET /api/route/list?prefix=...
      Dispatch lives in the server boundary after the static matchers;
      grants on publish/delete follow the existing prefix-grant model
      (admin bypass). |))))
  (subsection acceptance
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      1. PUBLISH WITHOUT REDEPLOY. A template route published via the
         API serves byte-identical content to an anonymous caller
         through the public front with the record's content_type.
      2. PROGRAM ROUTE. A stored program runs per request: journaled,
         attributable run row; the request context reaches the program
         as inputs; result bytes are served per the contract.
      3. SHADOW REFUSED. Publishing a route over a reserved path is a
         journaled denial; the static surface still answers.
      4. CAPABILITY CHECK. Publish/delete without a covering grant is a
         journaled denial.
      5. LIFECYCLE. Deleted route answers 404 immediately; rewind over
         a prefix containing route/ restores the prior route table
         exactly (rewind-as-fold covers routes because routes are
         paths). |))))))
